    "title": "Shreveport's Clay Meyer: Fire captain, nurse, making a difference - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Mya Alysia ,\n57 minutes ago\nSHREVEPORT, La. ( KTAL/KMSS ) — Shreveport Fire Capt. Clay Meyer didn’t just choose a life of service; he was born into it. Meyer, who has spent 20 years with the Shreveport Fire Department, grew up watching his father work as a firefighter. He carries on that legacy as a captain, and his resume doesn’t stop there.\nSince 2016, Meyer has worked as a nurse, and he has served in the Air Force Reserve for nearly 19 years, where he handled both transportation and nursing duties.\n“Well, I think being a nurse, I kind of get to see after the fact what happens when we take someone to the hospital, so I always try to do as much as I can,” Meyer said. “That makes it better for the patient once they get to the hospital. In the military, the same thing, nursing is still taking care of patients.”\nAs a captain, Meyer oversees and manages the safety of his crew. Most of his years on the job have been spent on the medic unit.\n“He has a good rapport with the public, a good sense of humor when it comes to what we do on a day-to-day basis,” Casey said. “He’s just been an all-around firefighter and officer.”\nAfter two decades on the job, Meyer says he has about nine more years before he retires. Until then, he’ll keep showing up.\nStay up to date with the latest news, weather and sports by downloading the WGNO app on the Apple or Google Play stores and by subscribing to the WGNO newsletter .\nLatest Posts Copyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WGNO.",
